<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Port-Royal (Acadia)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1605">1605</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Port-Royal was the capital of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>, founded in 1605 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-dugua-de-mons">Pierre Dugua de Mons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> on the Annapolis Basin in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>. Destroyed by English raiders in 1613, it was re-established nearby and changed hands several times. After its capture by British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> forces in 1710 it was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annapolis-royal">Annapolis Royal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/port-royal-acadia.jpg" alt="Port-Royal (Acadia)" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: attributed to Jean de Labat · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PortRoyalAcadia1702.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Champlain named the harbour Port Royal in 1604, judging that the sheltered basin on the south shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-fundy">Bay of Fundy</a> could hold several hundred ships of the French fleet, and for many years the name served for both the water and the settlements around it. From 1605 to 1613 the colony centred on the Habitation on the north side of the basin; from 1629 its centre was Fort Anne on the south side, where the Annapolis River meets Allain's Creek.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlers moved there from Saint Croix Island after the disastrous winter of 1604-1605 and built a log stockade with help from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a> and their chief Membertou. Champlain founded the Order of Good Cheer, Marc Lescarbot's The Theatre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neptune">Neptune</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> was performed on 14 November 1606, and Louis Hébert grew vines, fruit trees and cereals there. The Habitation was left to Membertou from 1607 until Poutrincourt returned in 1610.</p><p>Samuel Argall sacked and burned the Habitation in 1613, and the settlement was re-established 8 km upstream on the south bank. Scottish settlers under William Alexander the younger built Charles Fort in 1629, handed back under the Treaty of Saint Germain-en-Laye in 1632. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-menou-daulnay">Charles de Menou d'Aulnay</a> made Port-Royal his capital, built the first dykes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and fought Charles de la Tour in a civil war that he won in 1645.</p><p>Robert Sedgwick took the town in 1654 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> militia and soldiers sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>, and the English held it until the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> of 1667; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1671 counted 361 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadians">Acadians</a>. Sir William Phips captured it in 1690, two sieges in 1707 failed against Governor Daniel d'Auger de Subercase, and in 1710 a British force of 36 ships and 2,000 men took it after a siege ending on 2 October. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> ceded it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> in 1713.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annapolis-royal">Annapolis Royal</a> stands on the site. Including a raid by Americans during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, the place faced thirteen attacks, more than any other in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. A replica of the original Habitation stands on the north shore of the Annapolis Basin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Port-Royal (Acadia)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a first settlement at <b>Port-Royal</b> in Acadia in 1605, Samuel de Champlain founded Quebec in 1608 as a fur-trading post, allied himself with the Montagnais, Algonquin and Huron, and explored as far as Lake Huron.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1604 Pierre Dugua de Mons and Samuel de Champlain wintered on Saint Croix Island, and in 1605 moved across the Bay of Fundy to found <b>Port-Royal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/acadia">Acadia</a></li></ul></section>
